@Bill

Apparently in many Romance and Western languages the moon is feminine for mythological reasons (lunar deities were female). In Slavic languages there was no religious background for the name. What I managed to find on the Polish internet is this: originally "księżyc" was only the designation of the new moon. Back then the word meant "little prince" or "son of a prince", and our ancestors called the new moon "little prince" because it was up high yet tiny. Over time the name came to mean the moon as such, not just one of its phases. Apparently it is not even certain that pagan Slavs actually worshipped any lunar deity. There are theories they might have (the candidate, Chors, or Khors, was male), but they have not been universally accepted. Thus, unlike in many Western countries with their links to ancient Greek/Roman female lunar deities, in Poland there is no natural connection between femininity and the moon.

The sun, słońce, is actually neuter in Polish, so neither male nor female. The simple reason is that nouns ending in -e are generally neuter in Polish. The etymology of "słońce" is extremely complex, going back to protoindoeuropean roots, but apparently ultimately what mattered was how the word loaned from other languages fit into Polish grammar - as it was spelled, with that E at the end, it made sense for it to be neuter.

