I volunteer in the ER. Sometimes on my way to work, I'll be behind an ambulance, and they don't have their lights/sirens on. Only once...in fact, last week...did they use them, in my three years there, during my commute. But meanwhile, in the hospital you hear two beeps, then "Alert: Adult trauma 1 ETA 5 minutes" or whatever it is. And in a matter of seconds, the trauma staff is assembling (get out of their way, STAT!), registration is entering an anonymous person (the name will come later), and communication is happening between the ambulance and the hospital so the medical staff know what to expect and who needs to be on hand, medically. I call it the ER ballet, as I watch staff practice a choreography of quickly moving personnel, beds, and machines, around visitors, housekeeping staff, dining carts, and other everyday happenings. 

I love volunteering in the ER because I see miracles every day.

